*---------------------------------------------------------------
* startup.equ -- process-startup convention: where argc/argv
* live at process entry. RATIFIED, docs/SYSCALL.md ruling 13
* (item 13, "argv staging") -- both placement AND content shape,
* closing this out entirely; this file's convention is what the
* kernel actually implements now, not a proposal it might
* revise.
* Full design notes: L.STARTUPE.TXT (docs/a2/)
*---------------------------------------------------------------

* 2026-08-17 (docs/LIMITS.md): these now DERIVE from the
* canonical argv-block addresses in inc/memmap.equ instead
* of recomputing the layout from PRC_LOWFREE. Both sides used to
* describe the same three addresses independently -- this file
* from PRC_LOWFREE, the kernel from its own copies in
* process.s -- and that is the mechanism by which the two layers
* came to disagree about what happens when argv is too big (the
* kernel truncated silently, sh errored). One layout, one place.
*
* The limit is the kernel's, and it is in BYTES: ARG_MAX (1280)
* of packed strings plus ARGV_PTR_LIM (255) of pointer array,
* both derived from the memory map. spawn() returns ERR_2BIG
* rather than staging a prefix, so a program can trust that if
* it started at all, its ARGC/ARGV are complete.
*
* THE ENVIRONMENT LIVES HERE TOO (docs/SYSCALL.md item 13c,
* 2026-08-22). It is not a second block: envc follows argv's
* pointer array and envp's own pointers follow envc, all inside
* ARGV, and envp's strings follow argv's inside PRC_ARGV_STR --
* so ARG_MAX and ARGV_PTR_LIM are now budgets for the PAIR.
*
*   ARGC                     argc
*   ARGV+0                   argv[0..argc-1], 2 bytes each
*   ARGV+2*argc              envc
*   ARGV+2*argc+1            envp[0..envc-1], 2 bytes each
*
* envc's address depends on argc, so it is NOT an equ: read it
* with src/liba/env.s's GETENV, which does that arithmetic once
* and is the only thing that should. A program the kernel
* spawned always has a real envc byte, zero when the parent
* passed no environment.
ARGC        equ   PRC_ARGV_HDR   ; 1 byte
ARGV        equ   PRC_ARGV_PTRS  ; argc * 2 bytes
ARGVPAGE    equ   PRC_ARGV_HDR   ;kept: older code names the
                                 ;block by its page
